<article class="env-lemma" id="09UV">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/09UV">Lemma <span data-tag="09UV">5.13.4</span>.</a> Let $X$ be a Hausdorff and quasi-compact space. Let $X = \bigcup _{i \in I} U_ i$ be an open covering. Then there exists an open covering $X = \bigcup _{i \in I} V_ i$ such that $\overline{V_ i} \subset U_ i$ for all $i$. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Let $x \in X$. Choose an $i(x) \in I$ such that $x \in U_{i(x)}$. Since $X \setminus U_{i(x)}$ and $\{ x\} $ are disjoint closed subsets of $X$, by Lemmas <a href="/tag/005C" data-tag="005C">5.12.3</a> and <a href="/tag/08YB" data-tag="08YB">5.12.4</a> there exists an open neighbourhood $U_ x$ of $x$ whose closure is disjoint from $X \setminus U_{i(x)}$. Thus $\overline{U_ x} \subset U_{i(x)}$. Since $X$ is quasi-compact, there is a finite list of points $x_1, \ldots , x_ m$ such that $X = U_{x_1} \cup \ldots \cup U_{x_ m}$. Setting $V_ i = \bigcup _{i = i(x_ j)} U_{x_ j}$ the proof is finished. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>